

**29. Laplace Transforms**

**29.1. Definition of the Laplace Transform**

**One-dimensional Laplace Transform**

**29.1.1**: \(f(s)\!=\!\mathscr{L}\!\left\langle\,F(t)\,\right\rangle\!=\!\!\int_{0}^{s}e^{-s t}F(t)dt\)__

\(F(t)\) is a function of the real variable \(t\) and \(s\) is a complex variable. \(F(t)\) is called the original function and \(f(s)\) is called the image function. If the integral in **29.1.1** converges for a real \(s\!=\!\!s_{0}\), i.e.,

\[\lim_{\begin{subarray}{c}A\to 0\\ B\to a\end{subarray}}\!\!\int_{A}^{B}e^{-t\phi}F(t)dt\]

exists, then it converges for all \(s\) with \(\mathscr{R}s\!>\!\!s_{0}\), and the image function is a single valued analytic function of \(s\) in the half-plane \(\mathscr{R}s\!>\!\!s_{0}\).

**Two-dimensional Laplace Transform**

**29.1.2**: \(f(u,v)\!=\!\mathscr{L}\!\left\langle\,F(x,y)\,\right\rangle\!=\!\!\int_{0}^{ \infty}\!\!\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-ux-vF}(x,y)dxdy\)__

**Definition of the Unit Step Function**

**29.1.3**: \(u(t)\!=\!\left\{\begin{array}{ll}0&(t\!<\!0)\\ \frac{1}{2}&(t\!=\!0)\\ 1&(t\!>\!0)\end{array}\right.\)__

In the following tables the factor \(u(t)\) is to be understood as multiplying the original function \(F(t)\).

